From e0de67d585e370f27625cb29230390a6f0ac085b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Mon, 13 Jul 2026 14:02:29 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] theme: move the Zeo borders outside the surface ladder

A regression this story introduced, not a defect it inherited. Upstream One's
ladder is flat -- surface and elevated are byte-identical -- so one fixed
border.variant sat consistently above all of it and could not cross anything.
Zeo built a real four-rung ladder and left both border tokens exactly where One
had them. border.variant is now lighter than the surface rung, byte-adjacent to
the elevated rung, and darker than the modal rung: it crosses the ladder instead
of clearing it.

Against the elevated surface it scores 1.00:1. The hairline is not faint there,
it is absent -- and StyledExt::elevated paints border_variant on every elevated
surface, so this is not one widget but all 71 call sites: every picker, command
palette, popover, context menu and toast. R2.1 asks for a background step AND a
hairline to separate an elevated surface from the one beneath. What shipped had
only the step.

Nothing caught it because nothing asserted it. design.md required boundaries to
clear a contrast floor, but that clause narrowed to "the accent clears AA" when
it was transcribed into the task plan, so no test ever measured a border against
a surface. All seven pre-authored tests were green through the whole regression.
The test added here is property-based -- it reads whatever hexes the palette
carries and computes the contrast, so it cannot be satisfied by pinning a value
-- and it sweeps both tokens against every rung in both variants, sixteen pairs,
accumulating all violations rather than short-circuiting on the first. It was
confirmed red before the fix, at 1.00:1 on the elevated pair, and re-proved by
mutation afterwards.

The floors are perceptibility floors, deliberately low: border 1.5:1,
border.variant 1.25:1. The 3:1 that WCAG 1.4.11 mandates governs focus rings and
UI components, not surface separators; border.focused already clears it at
3.77-5.81:1 and is now pinned by its own sweep. A hairline is supposed to be a
hairline -- it only has to exist.

Two floors rather than one, on purpose. A single shared floor drives both tokens
to the same colour and destroys the prominence hierarchy that 162 border and 127
border_variant call sites consume, so the two are held distinct by a test as
well.

The values move only in lightness -- hue and saturation are preserved, dark
borders up and light borders down, outward from the ladder in both directions --
and the four surface rungs are untouched. The equilibrium is tight by design:
four of the sixteen pairs clear their floor by under 0.003, all of them at the
ladder's extremes, which is the unavoidable geometry of one token spanning four
rungs. Any future repaint of the canvas or modal rung will flip the sweep red
without a border being touched. That is the sweep working, not the sweep being
brittle.

Covers R2.1, R3.9, R3.10.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 assets/themes/zeo/zeo.json               |   8 +-
 crates/theme_settings/tests/zeo_theme.rs | 212 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 216 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

